  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 155 AM MDT Sat Jul 11 2026\n\nWeekend still looks very dry. Although the models are trying to\nsuggest a rogue storm possible Saturday over the hills south of\nBurley and also Sunday across the eastern highlands and central\nmountains. The issue is the position of the center of the ridge\nwhich has the very dry air with the upcoming monsoon moisture \nthat will work up from the south as the ridge moves east. \nProbability of actually developing a dry storm is probably 10 \npercent or less. Monday continues to look dry as well, although \nsuspect we'll be seeing similar types of issues once we start \ngetting into the time frame of the higher resolution models. The\nweekend still looks very hot with near record or record highs. \nUpper 90s to near 100 is likely for most lower elevations today,\nwith upper 90s to lower 100s on Sunday, and upper 90s to around\n100 or so on Monday. Heat advisories and warnings remain in \neffect for most locations with the heat risk indicating that the\nonly real reprieve is from Ketchum up to Stanley. It will be \nbreezy over the weekend. Most of the winds are from the central \nmountains into the upper Snake Rive Plain on Saturday and more \nareawide on Sunday. On one hand, the breeze might make someone \nfeel a bit cooler. But on the other hand, one could potentially \ndehydrate faster. \n\nThe monsoon push arrives Tuesday. The added clouds/storms will help \nkeep highs a touch lower. But temps will remain solidly in the 90s \nfor next week, especially in areas that dont see appreciable clouds \nor storms.",
